Guided Exercise: Kubernetes Pod and Service Networks
Goal of the Lab
You will:
1.	Deploy a MySQL database pod
2.	Create a Kubernetes Service
3.	Access the database using the service DNS name
4.	Test pod-to-pod networking
5.	Use a job to initialize the database
This demonstrates how pods communicate using services instead of direct IP addresses.
________________________________________
Step 1 — Start the Lab Environment
lab start deploy-services
Purpose
This command:
•	prepares the OpenShift cluster
•	creates the project deploy-services
•	creates a file:
/home/student/DO180/labs/deploy-services/resources.txt
This file contains helpful commands for the lab.
________________________________________
Step 2 — Login to the Cluster
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
login	authenticate to cluster
-u developer	username
-p developer	password
________________________________________
Step 3 — Use the Correct Project
oc project deploy-services
Projects in OpenShift are namespaces used to organize resources.
________________________________________
Step 4 — Create MySQL Deployment
oc create deployment db-pod --port 3306 \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel8/mysql-80
Explanation
Parameter	Meaning
db-pod	deployment name
--port 3306	MySQL port
--image	container image
The container image runs MySQL server.
________________________________________
Step 5 — Add Required Environment Variables
MySQL containers require configuration variables.
oc set env deployment/db-pod \
MYSQL_USER=user1 \
MYSQL_PASSWORD=mypa55w0rd \
MYSQL_DATABASE=items
What these variables do
Variable	Purpose
MYSQL_USER	database user
MYSQL_PASSWORD	user password
MYSQL_DATABASE	database name
________________________________________
Step 6 — Confirm Pod is Running
oc get pods
Example output:
db-pod-6ccc485cfc-vrc4r   Running
Meaning:
•	Deployment created a pod
•	MySQL container started successfully
________________________________________
Step 7 — Create a Kubernetes Service
Now expose the deployment.
oc expose deployment/db-pod
This creates a ClusterIP service.
________________________________________
What the Service Does
Instead of accessing the pod directly:
Pod → Pod IP
We access:
Pod → Service → Pod
Service acts as a stable network endpoint.
________________________________________
Step 8 — Verify the Service
oc get service db-pod -o wide
Example output:
NAME     TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      PORT   SELECTOR
db-pod   ClusterIP   172.30.108.92   3306   app=db-pod
Important fields:
Field	Meaning
Cluster-IP	stable internal IP
Port	service port
Selector	which pods belong to service
Selector:
app=db-pod
________________________________________
Step 9 — Verify Pod Labels
Capture pod name:
PODNAME=$(oc get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
Check labels:
oc get pod $PODNAME --show-labels
Example:
LABELS
app=db-pod
This matches the service selector.
________________________________________
Step 10 — View Service Endpoints
oc get endpoints
Example:
NAME     ENDPOINTS
db-pod   10.8.0.85:3306
Endpoint = pod IP + port used by service.
________________________________________
Step 11 — Verify Pod IP
oc get pods -o wide
Example:
db-pod-xxxx   Running   10.8.0.85
The endpoint matches the pod IP.
________________________________________
Step 12 — Delete the Deployment
oc delete deployment db-pod
Now check services.
oc get service
Output:
db-pod   ClusterIP
The service still exists.
________________________________________
Step 13 — Check Endpoints
oc get endpoints
Output:
db-pod   <none>
Because there are no pods running.
________________________________________
Step 14 — Recreate the Deployment
oc create deployment db-pod --port 3306 \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel8/mysql-80
Add environment variables again:
oc set env deployment/db-pod \
MYSQL_USER=user1 \
MYSQL_PASSWORD=mypa55w0rd \
MYSQL_DATABASE=items
________________________________________
Step 15 — Verify Service Automatically Updates
Check pods:
oc get pods --selector app=db-pod -o wide
Example:
db-pod-xxxxx   Running   10.8.0.90
Check endpoints:
oc get endpoints
Example:
db-pod   10.8.0.90:3306
Service automatically updated.
________________________________________
Step 16 — Create a Troubleshooting Pod
Create a pod named shell.
oc run shell -it \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/openshift4/network-tools-rhel8
This pod contains network troubleshooting tools.
________________________________________
Step 17 — Check DNS Configuration
Inside the pod:
cat /etc/resolv.conf
Example:
search deploy-services.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local
nameserver 172.30.0.10
Meaning:
•	Kubernetes internal DNS server handles service names.
DNS server used:
•	CoreDNS
________________________________________
Step 18 — Test Service DNS
Run:
nc -z db-pod.deploy-services 3306
If connection succeeds:
Connection success
Meaning:
Pod → Service DNS → MySQL Pod
________________________________________
Step 19 — Delete Troubleshooting Pod
Exit shell:
exit
Delete pod:
oc delete pod shell
________________________________________
Step 20 — Cross-Namespace Networking
Create new namespace:
oc new-project deploy-services-2
Test DNS connection:
oc run shell -it --rm \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/openshift4/network-tools-rhel8 \
--restart Never \
-- nc -z db-pod.deploy-services.svc.cluster.local 3306
Notice the full DNS name:
db-pod.deploy-services.svc.cluster.local
________________________________________
Step 21 — Return to Original Project
oc project deploy-services
________________________________________
Step 22 — Initialize the Database
Create a job:
oc create job mysql-init \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/do180-dbinit:v1 \
-- /bin/bash -c "mysql -uuser1 -pmypa55w0rd \
--protocol tcp -h db-pod -P3306 items </tmp/db-init.sql"
This job:
1.	Connects to MySQL
2.	Runs SQL script
3.	Creates a table
4.	Inserts data
________________________________________
Step 23 — Check Job Status
oc get job
Example:
mysql-init   Complete
________________________________________
Step 24 — Delete the Job
oc delete job mysql-init
________________________________________
Step 25 — Query the Database
Create a temporary client pod:
oc run query-db -it --rm \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/do180-dbinit:v1 \
--restart Never \
-- mysql -uuser1 -pmypa55w0rd \
-h db-pod -P3306 items -e 'select * from Item;'
Output:
1  Pick up newspaper
2  Buy groceries
Meaning the database initialization worked.
________________________________________
Step 26 — Pod-to-Pod Troubleshooting
Find pod IP:
oc get pods -o wide
Example:
10.8.0.69
Save it:
POD_IP=$(oc get pod -l app=db-pod \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.podIP}')
________________________________________
Step 27 — Test Direct Pod Connection
oc run shell --env POD_IP=$POD_IP -it --rm \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/openshift4/network-tools-rhel8 \
--restart Never \
-- nc -z $POD_IP 3306
If successful:
Connection success
This confirms pod-to-pod networking works.
________________________________________
Step 28 — Finish the Lab
lab finish deploy-services
This cleans up cluster resources.
________________________________________
Key Concepts Learned
Pods
•	each pod gets a dynamic IP
•	pods can restart anytime
________________________________________
Services
Provide:
•	stable IP
•	load balancing
•	service discovery
________________________________________
DNS
Pods access services using:
service-name
or
service-name.namespace
